WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.978
Imagine your data flagging problems for you, automatically.

2
00:00:03.978 --> 00:00:09.541
In this lesson, you will learn to write IF in a calculated column, combine

3
00:00:09.541 --> 00:00:13.116
conditions with AND, and use OR for either-or rules.

4
00:00:13.116 --> 00:00:15.500
Let's put your data to work!

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.960
In Table view, on the Shipments table, I click New column.

6
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.926
The column is SP01 Cancelled.

7
00:00:30.926 --> 00:00:32.548
IF has three parts:

8
00:00:32.548 --> 00:00:37.006
a test, the value if true, and the value if false.

9
00:00:37.006 --> 00:00:41.059
If the order status is Cancelled, return Ask the Reason.

10
00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:42.680
Otherwise, return Leave It.

11
00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.973
Now let's add a second condition:

12
00:00:58.973 --> 00:01:01.736
the salesperson must be S P zero one.

13
00:01:01.736 --> 00:01:04.845
To test two things at once, we use AND.

14
00:01:04.845 --> 00:01:08.300
AND is true only when all its conditions are true.

15
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:23.596
Careful here:

16
00:01:23.596 --> 00:01:28.470
the AND function takes only two arguments, and it must sit inside the IF

17
00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.211
test, before the first comma.

18
00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:34.040
Putting it in the wrong place is a very common error.

19
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.958
Here's a shortcut: the double ampersand.

20
00:01:50.958 --> 00:01:55.760
It means the same as AND, and it can join more than two conditions.

21
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.913
For either-or rules, use OR, or the double pipe.

22
00:02:17.913 --> 00:02:21.260
OR is true when at least one condition is true.

23
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.862
Flags like this save hours.

24
00:02:46.862 --> 00:02:53.533
In monitoring work, the same idea catches late reports, missing data, or sites that need a visit.

25
00:02:53.533 --> 00:02:56.280
Now try one with your own data!

